<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="981add89" lines="10-29">
<description>
Extend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying
  one column and one ray color.
- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.

Rule:
- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same
  column down to the bottom edge.
- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.
- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.
- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.

Key insight:
The top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells
matching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="8a6d367c" lines="52-67">
<description>
Complete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.

Input structure:
- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.
- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.
- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.

Rule:
- Crop to the rectangular frame.
- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.
- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.

Key insight:
The lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="2c181942" lines="7-21">
<description>
Attach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).
- 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.

Rule:
- For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.
- Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).
- Translate it to adjoin that hub side. 
- Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.

Key insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="78e78cff" lines="71-91">
<description>
Recolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.
- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.
- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.

Rule:
- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly
  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only
  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.
- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.
- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.
- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.

Key insight:
- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the
  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling
  through connected background.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="3dc255db" lines="37-53">
<description>
Eject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.
- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.
- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.

Rule:
- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.
- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.
- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,
  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.

Key insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite
the mouth opening where the content lives.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="1478ab18" lines="35-43">
<description>
Paint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.

Input structure
The grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.
Rule
Find the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.
Key insight
The present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="973e499e" lines="7-22">
<description>
Expand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.

Input structure
- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.
- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.
- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.

Rule
- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.
- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.
- Write black everywhere else in the block.

Key insight
Each pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="f560132c" lines="259-273">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the "pieces"), scattered across the grid.
- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.

Rule:
- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.
- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.
- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.

Key insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="33067df9" lines="32-48">
<description>
Expand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.
- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.

Rule
- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.
- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.
- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.
- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.
- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.

Key insight
- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="230f2e48" lines="19-38">
<description>
Fold each straight red rod at its black marker, leaving the gray-anchored side fixed.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Each foreground object is a straight horizontal or vertical 4-connected rod.
- Each rod contains many pixels of one line color, one endpoint marker, and one
  interior control marker.

Rule:
- For every rod, keep the segment from the endpoint marker through the control
  marker unchanged.
- Move the line-color tail on the far side of the control marker to a
  perpendicular ray starting next to that marker.
- Choose the perpendicular direction with more space to the grid edge.

Key insight:
- The endpoint marker identifies the fixed side of the rod, while the control
  marker is the hinge; the tail folds into the larger open half-plane.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="c920a713" lines="87-102">
<description>
Untangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.

Input structure
- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.
- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.
- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.

Rule
- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.
- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.
- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.

Key insight
- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

